<DocsLayout
  title="Quick Start — PostgreSQL Schema Designer"
  description="Get started with PgDesigner in 5 minutes. Install via brew, create tables with visual ERD, generate production-ready SQL, lint your schema, and commit to git.">

<p>This guide takes you from zero to a working schema in 5 minutes.</p>

<h2>1. Install</h2>

<pre><code>brew tap vmkteam/tap
brew install pgdesigner</code></pre>

<p>Or <a href="/docs/download">download the binary</a> for your platform.</p>

<h2>2. Open the visual editor</h2>

<pre><code>pgdesigner</code></pre>

<p>This starts a local server and opens PgDesigner in your browser. The Welcome Screen offers five sample databases — or click <strong>New Project</strong>.</p>

<p>The Project Settings dialog opens. Name the project (e.g., <code>shop</code>) and set <strong>Naming → Convention</strong> to <code>snake_case</code> — the default is <code>camelCase</code>. Click <strong>Save</strong>.</p>

<h2>3. Create your first table</h2>

<ol>
  <li>Click <strong>⊞T</strong> in the toolbar (or press <kbd>T</kbd>), then click an empty spot on the canvas</li>
  <li>Enter the table name — <code>users</code></li>
  <li>The Table Editor opens with a primary key already in place: <code>user_id integer</code>, an identity column</li>
  <li>Click <strong>+ Add</strong>, type <code>email</code>, press <kbd>Tab</kbd>, type <code>varchar(255)</code>, press <kbd>Enter</kbd></li>
  <li>Add <code>created_at</code> with type <code>timestamptz</code> and set <strong>Default</strong> to <code>now()</code> in the side panel</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Save</strong></li>
</ol>

<p>New columns are <code>NOT NULL</code> by default — that's a project setting too.</p>

<h2>4. Add a second table with a foreign key</h2>

<ol>
  <li>Create another table the same way — <code>orders</code>. It gets <code>order_id</code> as its primary key</li>
  <li>Click <strong>→FK</strong> in the toolbar (or press <kbd>F</kbd>)</li>
  <li>Click <code>orders</code>, then click <code>users</code></li>
  <li>PgDesigner adds a <code>user_id</code> column to <code>orders</code>, the <code>fk_orders_user_id</code> foreign key, and an <code>ix_orders_user_id</code> index on it</li>
</ol>

